    <h2>What an upgrade includes</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>A new panel sized for your home, usually 200 amps, with space to spare.</li>
        <li>New breakers, including AFCI and GFCI protection where today's code calls for it.</li>
        <li>A new meter base and service connection when the utility requires one.</li>
        <li>Grounding and bonding brought up to code.</li>
        <li>The permit, the inspection and coordination with the utility.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>Panels we replace on sight</h2>
    <p>Some older panels have breakers that can fail to trip under overload. If we find one of those brands, we will show you why we recommend replacing it, even if you only called about something else.</p>
    <h2>Planning for the next ten years</h2>
    <p>Before we size a panel we ask what is on your list: a heat pump, an EV, a hot tub, a workshop. A load calculation on paper tells us whether 200 amps is enough, or whether a smart load manager can avoid a bigger service altogether.</p>
</ServicePage>
